From: Seeed Rockchip BSP
Subject: [PATCH] drm: guard drm_getunique() against a NULL master

Only primary-node file opens get a drm_master assigned;
drm_file_alloc() creates none and drm_open_helper() calls
drm_master_open() for primary clients only, so a render-node fd
carries file_priv->master == NULL by design.  Upstream never reaches
drm_getunique() with such an fd: drm_ioctl_permit() rejects
DRM_IOCTL_GET_UNIQUE (flags 0, not DRM_RENDER_ALLOW) for render
clients with -EACCES.

The vendor tree ships CONFIG_DRM_IGNORE_IOTCL_PERMIT=y (set in
rockchip_linux_defconfig), which compiles the entire permission
check out.  A render-node fd then reaches drm_getunique() and
dereferences the NULL master:

    Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at virtual address 0000000000000018
    pc : drm_getunique+0x34/0xd8
    Call trace: drm_getunique <- drm_ioctl_kernel <- drm_ioctl <- __arm64_sys_ioctl

observed on the RK3576 DevKit when Xorg's glamor bring-up probed
/dev/dri/renderD128 (libdrm drmGetBusid() path).  Return -EACCES
for a NULL master, matching what the compiled-out permit check
would have answered, instead of oopsing the caller.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [vendor config: CONFIG_DRM_IGNORE_IOTCL_PERMIT]
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c	2026-09-24 17:20:18.003493974 +0800
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c	2026-09-24 17:20:18.027918024 +0800
@@ -121,6 +121,10 @@

 	mutex_lock(&dev->master_mutex);
 	master = file_priv->master;
+	if (!master) {
+		mutex_unlock(&dev->master_mutex);
+		return -EACCES;
+	}
 	if (u->unique_len >= master->unique_len) {
 		if (copy_to_user(u->unique, master->unique, master->unique_len)) {
 			mutex_unlock(&dev->master_mutex);
